<div class="analogy reveal">
  <p>Sequential is like calling three friends one after another — call Alice, hang up, call Bob, hang up, call Carol. Parallel is like sending all three a text at once and waiting for whoever replies last. Same information, much less waiting.</p>
</div>

<!-- SECTION 06: THE #1 MISTAKE -->
<section class="section reveal">
  <span class="section-num part4-num">06</span>
  <h2>The #1 Mistake: <code>async def</code> + Blocking Code</h2>
  <p>This is the most common async mistake in FastAPI, and it's <strong>silent</strong>. No error, no warning — just a server that freezes under load.</p>
</section>

<div class="warning-block reveal">
  <div class="warning-header">
    <span class="warning-icon">&#9888;</span>
    <span class="warning-title">Silent performance killer — no error thrown</span>
  </div>
  <div class="code-pair">
    <div class="code-pair-block">
      <div class="code-pair-label bad">&#10007; ANTIPATTERN</div>
      <pre><span style="color:#a29bfe">@app.get</span>(<span style="color:#55efc4">"/bad"</span>)
<span style="color:#a29bfe">async def</span> <span style="color:#55efc4">bad_endpoint</span>():
    time.sleep(5)
    <span style="color:#636e72"># BLOCKS the event loop!</span>
    <span style="color:#636e72"># All other requests frozen</span>
    <span style="color:#636e72"># for 5 seconds.</span>

    result = requests.get(url)
    <span style="color:#636e72"># BLOCKS again!</span>
    <span style="color:#636e72"># Sync HTTP inside async def.</span>
    <span style="color:#a29bfe">return</span> result.json()</pre>
    </div>
    <div class="code-pair-block">
      <div class="code-pair-label good">&#10003; CORRECT VERSION</div>
      <pre><span style="color:#a29bfe">@app.get</span>(<span style="color:#55efc4">"/good"</span>)
<span style="color:#a29bfe">async def</span> <span style="color:#55efc4">good_endpoint</span>():
    <span style="color:#a29bfe">await</span> asyncio.sleep(5)
    <span style="color:#636e72"># pauses the coroutine,</span>
    <span style="color:#636e72"># not the thread</span>

    <span style="color:#a29bfe">async with</span> httpx.AsyncClient() <span style="color:#a29bfe">as</span> c:
        result = <span style="color:#a29bfe">await</span> c.get(url)
    <span style="color:#636e72"># non-blocking HTTP</span>
    <span style="color:#a29bfe">return</span> result.json()</pre>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<section class="section reveal">
  <p><code>time.sleep(5)</code> doesn't pause the coroutine — it freezes the <strong>entire thread</strong>. The event loop can't run anything else. Every other request is stuck waiting for those 5 seconds. 100 users hit this endpoint at the same time → 100 requests queued up → each waits for all the ones before it.</p>

  <h3 style="margin-top:2rem;font-size:1.1rem;color:#2d3436">The substitution rules</h3>
  <div class="code-block">
    <pre><span class="code-comment">In async def — swap blocking calls for async equivalents:</span>
  time.sleep(n)     → asyncio.sleep(n)
  requests.get(url) → httpx.AsyncClient().get(url)
  open(<span class="code-string">"file"</span>)      → aiofiles.open(<span class="code-string">"file"</span>)
  psycopg2 queries  → asyncpg queries

<span class="code-comment">If you must use a blocking library in async def:</span>
  → move it to a <span class="code-keyword">def</span> endpoint instead, let FastAPI thread-pool it
  → or wrap it: <span class="code-keyword">await</span> run_in_threadpool(blocking_func)</pre>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="analogy reveal">
  <p><code>time.sleep</code> in an <code>async def</code> is like the one employee with the to-do list deciding to take a nap instead of switching tasks. Everyone else on the list is frozen until they wake up.</p>
</div>

<!-- SECTION 07: HOW FASTAPI KNOWS -->
<section class="section reveal">
  <span class="section-num part4-num">07</span>
  <h2>How FastAPI Knows What to Do</h2>
  <p>When a request comes in, FastAPI inspects your endpoint function before calling it:</p>
</section>

<div class="decision-tree reveal">
  <!-- Top: request arrives -->
  <div class="dt-box dt-box-dark">
    <span class="dt-label" style="color:#a29bfe">request arrives</span>
    <span class="dt-desc" style="color:#dfe6e9">GET /users/42</span>
  </div>

  <!-- Connector -->
  <div class="dt-connector">
    <div class="dt-conn-line"></div>
    <div class="dt-conn-text">FastAPI inspects the handler</div>
    <div class="dt-conn-line"></div>
  </div>

  <!-- Decision -->
  <div class="dt-box dt-box-purple">
    <span class="dt-label" style="color:#6c5ce7">is it async def?</span>
    <span class="dt-desc" style="color:#5a4fcf">FastAPI checks whether the function is a coroutine (<code style="background:rgba(108,92,231,0.12);padding:0.1rem 0.3rem;border-radius:0.25rem">async def</code>) or a regular function (<code style="background:rgba(108,92,231,0.12);padding:0.1rem 0.3rem;border-radius:0.25rem">def</code>)</span>
  </div>

  <!-- Two branches -->
  <div class="dt-branches">
    <div class="dt-branch dt-branch-yes">
      <span class="dt-label" style="color:#00b894">YES</span>
      <span class="dt-desc" style="color:#00695c">runs directly on the event loop — each <code style="background:rgba(0,184,148,0.12);padding:0.1rem 0.25rem;border-radius:0.25rem">await</code> yields control back</span>
    </div>
    <div class="dt-branch dt-branch-no">
      <span class="dt-label" style="color:#0984e3">NO</span>
      <span class="dt-desc" style="color:#0a6eb0">offloaded to a thread pool via <code style="background:rgba(9,132,227,0.12);padding:0.1rem 0.25rem;border-radius:0.25rem">run_in_threadpool</code> — blocking is safe there</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<section class="section reveal">
  <p>The thread pool FastAPI uses is <code>anyio</code>'s default thread pool — a managed pool of OS threads. Your sync handler runs there, the event loop keeps humming.</p>

  <div class="code-block">
    <pre><span class="code-comment"># FastAPI runs this on the event loop</span>
<span class="code-keyword">@app.get</span>(<span class="code-string">"/async-endpoint"</span>)
<span class="code-keyword">async def</span> <span class="code-string">async_endpoint</span>():
    data = <span class="code-keyword">await</span> some_async_db.fetch()
    <span class="code-keyword">return</span> data

<span class="code-comment"># FastAPI runs this in a thread pool</span>
<span class="code-keyword">@app.get</span>(<span class="code-string">"/sync-endpoint"</span>)
<span class="code-keyword">def</span> <span class="code-string">sync_endpoint</span>():
    data = some_sync_db.fetch()   <span class="code-comment"># blocks its own thread, nothing else is affected</span>
    <span class="code-keyword">return</span> data</pre>
  </div>

  <p>FastAPI handles the routing decision automatically. But the <em>responsibility</em> of using the right libraries in the right kind of function is yours.</p>
</section>

<!-- Four-Rule Matrix -->
<div class="rule-matrix reveal">
  <div class="rule-card good">
    <span class="rule-badge good">&#10003; CORRECT</span>
    <h4><code>async def</code> + async library</h4>
    <p>The ideal path. <code>asyncpg</code>, <code>httpx</code>, <code>aiofiles</code>, <code>motor</code>. Awaitable calls let the event loop breathe. Fast, correct, scales well.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="rule-card good">
    <span class="rule-badge good">&#10003; CORRECT</span>
    <h4><code>def</code> + sync library</h4>
    <p>Also correct. FastAPI runs it in a thread pool. <code>psycopg2</code>, <code>requests</code>, SQLAlchemy sync all work here. Slightly more memory (thread per request), but safe.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="rule-card good" style="background:#f0f8ff;border-color:#b3d9f7">
    <span class="rule-badge" style="background:rgba(9,132,227,0.12);color:#0984e3;border:0.0625rem solid rgba(9,132,227,0.25)">~ OK</span>
    <h4><code>def</code> + async library</h4>
    <p>Works, but you lose the async benefit. The async library is used inside a thread, so you get neither coroutine efficiency nor clean async flow. Avoid when possible.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="rule-card bad">
    <span class="rule-badge bad">&#10007; DANGER</span>
    <h4><code>async def</code> + sync library</h4>
    <p>Looks fine at first. Silently kills performance under load. Blocking calls (<code>time.sleep</code>, <code>requests.get</code>) freeze the entire event loop — all concurrent requests stall.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- RECAP -->
<section class="section reveal">
  <span class="section-num">RECAP</span>
  <h2>What We Learned</h2>

  <div style="display:grid;gap:1rem;margin-top:1.25rem">
    <div style="background:#f0efff;padding:1.25rem 1.5rem;border-radius:0.875rem;border-left:0.25rem solid #6c5ce7">
      <strong style="color:#6c5ce7">A coroutine is a pauseable function</strong> — defined with <code>async def</code>, it can suspend at <code>await</code> points and resume later without blocking the thread.
    </div>
    <div style="background:#e3f2fd;padding:1.25rem 1.5rem;border-radius:0.875rem;border-left:0.25rem solid #0984e3">
      <strong style="color:#0984e3">await suspends, not sleeps</strong> — control returns to the event loop, which runs other coroutines while this one waits for I/O.
    </div>
    <div style="background:#e8f8f5;padding:1.25rem 1.5rem;border-radius:0.875rem;border-left:0.25rem solid #00b894">
      <strong style="color:#00b894">Coroutines vs threads</strong> — coroutines are kilobytes each and cooperative; threads are ~8MB each on Linux (1MB on Windows) and preemptive. 10,000 coroutines = fine. 10,000 threads = server crash.
    </div>
    <div style="background:#fef5e7;padding:1.25rem 1.5rem;border-radius:0.875rem;border-left:0.25rem solid #e17055">
      <strong style="color:#e17055">FastAPI routes automatically</strong> — <code>async def</code> handlers run on the event loop; <code>def</code> handlers run in a thread pool via <code>run_in_threadpool</code>.
    </div>
    <div style="background:#fff5f5;padding:1.25rem 1.5rem;border-radius:0.875rem;border-left:0.25rem solid #e74c3c">
      <strong style="color:#e74c3c">asyncio.gather runs I/O concurrently</strong> — instead of waiting sequentially (sum of all times), independent coroutines are interleaved on the same thread and finish when the slowest completes. This is concurrency (interleaving), not parallelism (multiple cores).
    </div>
    <div style="background:#f8f0ff;padding:1.25rem 1.5rem;border-radius:0.875rem;border-left:0.25rem solid #9b59b6">
      <strong style="color:#9b59b6">The #1 mistake: blocking inside async def</strong> — <code>time.sleep</code>, <code>requests.get</code>, and sync DB drivers inside <code>async def</code> freeze the entire event loop silently. Use async equivalents or move to a <code>def</code> handler.
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- QUIZ -->
<section class="section reveal quiz-ep4">
  <span class="section-num part4-num">CHECKPOINT</span>
  <h2>Probe Your Thinking</h2>
  <p class="quiz-subtitle">No pressure. Just see what stuck.</p>

  <div class="quiz-grid">

  <!-- Q1 -->
  <div class="quiz-card">
    <div class="quiz-card-header">
      <span class="quiz-q-num">Q 01</span>
    </div>
    <p class="quiz-question">What does calling an <code>async def</code> function return — without using <code>await</code>?</p>
    <ul class="quiz-options">
      <li><button class="quiz-option" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">A</span>The function's return value</button></li>
      <li><button class="quiz-option" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">B</span><code>None</code></button></li>
      <li><button class="quiz-option" data-correct="true" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">C</span>A coroutine object</button></li>
      <li><button class="quiz-option" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">D</span>A <code>Future</code> object</button></li>
    </ul>
    <button class="quiz-reveal-btn" type="button" aria-expanded="false">Show Answer <span class="quiz-chevron">▾</span></button>
    <div class="quiz-answer">
      <div class="quiz-answer-inner">
        <div class="quiz-answer-highlight">✓ Correct: <strong>(C) A coroutine object</strong></div>
        <p class="quiz-answer-text"><code>async def</code> doesn't run the function immediately. It returns a coroutine object that must be awaited (or scheduled on the event loop) to actually execute. This is why forgetting <code>await</code> is such a common bug.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Q2 -->
  <div class="quiz-card">
    <div class="quiz-card-header">
      <span class="quiz-q-num">Q 02</span>
    </div>
    <p class="quiz-question">Running 10,000 coroutines simultaneously will crash a typical server.</p>
    <ul class="quiz-options">
      <li><button class="quiz-option" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">A</span>True</button></li>
      <li><button class="quiz-option" data-correct="true" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">B</span>False</button></li>
    </ul>
    <button class="quiz-reveal-btn" type="button" aria-expanded="false">Show Answer <span class="quiz-chevron">▾</span></button>
    <div class="quiz-answer">
      <div class="quiz-answer-inner">
        <div class="quiz-answer-highlight">✓ Correct: <strong>(B) False</strong></div>
        <p class="quiz-answer-text">Coroutines are incredibly lightweight, typically just a few kilobytes each. 10,000 coroutines is trivial. By contrast, 10,000 OS threads (each ~8MB stack on Linux) would need ~80GB of memory. This is precisely why async scales so well for I/O-bound workloads.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Q3 -->
  <div class="quiz-card">
    <div class="quiz-card-header">
      <span class="quiz-q-num">Q 03</span>
    </div>
    <p class="quiz-question">What's wrong with this FastAPI endpoint?</p>
    <div class="code-block" style="margin:0 0 1.25rem;max-width:100%">
      <pre>
<span class="code-keyword">@app</span>.get(<span class="code-string">"/data"</span>)
<span class="code-keyword">async def</span> get_data():
    result = requests.get(<span class="code-string">"https://api.example.com/data"</span>)
    <span class="code-keyword">return</span> result.json()</pre>
    </div>
    <ul class="quiz-options">
      <li><button class="quiz-option" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">A</span>The <code>requests</code> library isn't compatible with FastAPI</button></li>
      <li><button class="quiz-option" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">B</span>The URL format is invalid</button></li>
      <li><button class="quiz-option" data-correct="true" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">C</span>It blocks the event loop — should use an async HTTP client</button></li>
      <li><button class="quiz-option" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">D</span><code>async def</code> can't return JSON responses</button></li>
    </ul>
    <button class="quiz-reveal-btn" type="button" aria-expanded="false">Show Answer <span class="quiz-chevron">▾</span></button>
    <div class="quiz-answer">
      <div class="quiz-answer-inner">
        <div class="quiz-answer-highlight">✓ Correct: <strong>(C) It blocks the event loop</strong></div>
        <p class="quiz-answer-text"><code>requests.get()</code> is a synchronous, blocking HTTP call. Inside an <code>async def</code>, it freezes the entire event loop while waiting for the response — no other requests can be processed. The fix: use an async HTTP client like <code>httpx</code> with <code>await</code>, or change to a regular <code>def</code> so FastAPI runs it in a thread pool.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Q4 -->
  <div class="quiz-card">
    <div class="quiz-card-header">
      <span class="quiz-q-num">Q 04</span>
    </div>
    <p class="quiz-question">FastAPI sees an endpoint defined with plain <code>def</code> (not <code>async def</code>). What does it do?</p>
    <ul class="quiz-options">
      <li><button class="quiz-option" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">A</span>Rejects it — all endpoints must be <code>async def</code></button></li>
      <li><button class="quiz-option" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">B</span>Runs it directly on the event loop</button></li>
      <li><button class="quiz-option" data-correct="true" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">C</span>Runs it in a thread pool via <code>run_in_threadpool</code></button></li>
      <li><button class="quiz-option" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">D</span>Converts it to <code>async def</code> automatically</button></li>
    </ul>
    <button class="quiz-reveal-btn" type="button" aria-expanded="false">Show Answer <span class="quiz-chevron">▾</span></button>
    <div class="quiz-answer">
      <div class="quiz-answer-inner">
        <div class="quiz-answer-highlight">✓ Correct: <strong>(C) Runs it in a thread pool via <code>run_in_threadpool</code></strong></div>
        <p class="quiz-answer-text">FastAPI is smart about this. It detects that your function is synchronous and automatically runs it in a separate thread (using Starlette's <code>run_in_threadpool</code>, which wraps <code>anyio.to_thread.run_sync</code>) so it doesn't block the event loop. This is why plain <code>def</code> endpoints are safe for blocking code like database ORMs.</p>
      </div>
